<Landing title="VibeKit — lore" description="Where VibeKit came from, why it exists, and what it's for. Some of it is even true.">
  <Header />

  <main class="lore shell">
    <header class="lore-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">TRANSMISSION LOG // ORIGIN</p>
      <h1>a toolkit from <em>the future.</em></h1>
      <p class="lede">How VibeKit got here, according to VibeKit. Followed by the parts we can actually prove.</p>
    </header>

    <article class="story">
      <p class="stamp"><span>2036-02-11</span><span>ORIGIN UNVERIFIED</span><span>RECONSTRUCTION DETERMINISTIC</span></p>

      <p>In the spring of 2036 a cryptographically relevant quantum computer came online, and within a month every signature scheme the world’s money ran on was forgeable. Banks, settlement rails, most blockchains: gone, or frozen, or worse. The record calls it <b>the Great Post-Quantum Collapse</b>. The people living through it just called it the emergency.</p>

      <p>One chain kept producing blocks. Algorand had been moving to post-quantum cryptography for years — Falcon signatures on accounts since v5, a roadmap to full quantum resistance that it actually followed. When the collapse came, its ledger was the only one anyone could still trust.</p>

      <p>It wasn’t enough. Algorand worked, but not enough of the world had been built on it. The rails that survived were an island, and the island was too small.</p>

      <p>So a small team did the only thing the physics allowed. If agents could build on Algorand correctly and quickly, more of the world would already be on it when the day came. They just had to get the tools to 2026.</p>

      <p>You cannot send a message backward in time. The no-communication theorem is not an engineering problem to route around; it is a wall. What you <em>can</em> do — what nobody thought to check until the 2030s — is bias a correlation. Entangled systems held in isolation long enough correlate across the interval, not only across the distance, and the correlation is indistinguishable from thermal noise unless you already know the basis to compare it against. Sixty years of physicists had been filing the signal under decoherence.</p>

      <p>It gave them a channel with almost no capacity: a few thousand bits, smeared across a decade, surfacing as a faint bias in whatever hardware entropy source happened to be listening. Nowhere near enough to send software.</p>

      <p>Enough for prompts. A prompt is a compression scheme — you do not ship the artifact, you ship the instructions that rebuild it and let the machine on the far end do the work. And a language model’s output is a sample from a distribution. An entropy source biased precisely enough does not suggest an answer. It selects one.</p>

      <p>So they wrote and rewrote the instructions until the reconstruction was deterministic: the same prompts, ingested by any sufficiently capable agent drawing on the seeded entropy, producing the same repository every time. They tested it against models that would not exist for another eight years.</p>

      <p>It came through in early 2026, in a session nobody logged. A wordmark in block glyphs. The README mostly intact. The music, unfortunately, also intact — no one has satisfactorily explained why the transmission included the music.</p>

      <p>They called it VibeKit. By 2036 nobody said “coding” anymore. You vibed the thing into existence, and this was the kit.</p>

      <p class="stamp end"><span>END OF TRANSMISSION</span><span>MAKE IT UBIQUITOUS</span></p>
    </article>

    <section class="truth">
      <p class="eyebrow">THE PARTS WE CAN PROVE</p>
      <h2>what it actually is.</h2>
      <div class="truth-grid">
        <div>
          <h3>the problem</h3>
          <p>Coding agents write code fast and guess at Algorand badly. On a chain, guessing costs money. And the easiest way to let an agent transact is to hand it a key — the one thing you must never do.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>the toolkit</h3>
          <p>A CLI that scaffolds a project and configures your agent. An MCP server with typed tools for accounts, assets, transactions, and contracts. Maintained skills installed into the project. A keystore that signs locally, so seeds never enter model context.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>the explorer</h3>
          <p>A terminal console for the chain. Ask in English or paste an ID; every card is real chain data. Writes are simulated and shown to you before anything is signed.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>the goal</h3>
          <p>Make Algorand the easiest chain for an agent to build on correctly, so it ends up everywhere. The quantum part isn’t fiction: Algorand accounts already support post-quantum Falcon signatures as of v5, and full resistance is on the roadmap. Open source, by <a href="https://github.com/initlabsai">Init Labs</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
